{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n34 \n \n \nII.  Methods/Research Operations \n  \nA. Discussion of Method/Protocol \nFlame Design and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS \ncrew.  The crew\u2019s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests.  The \nmost common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) \nand the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. \n \nAll testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload \nOperations Center (GIPOC).  The science portion of the testing is typically conducted in a \nnominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts.  However, it may alternately \nbe conducted in a \u2018manual\u2019 mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in \nresponse to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry \nstream.  For example, the flow rate can be adjusted to approach a flame\u2019s soot limit.  This \nwas not done during Increments 59 and 60, where the console commanding during a test \nwas typically limited to halting the test when it was observed in the analog ops video that \nthe flame had extinguished. \n \nTesting is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to \nensure an acceptable acceleration environment. \n \nData is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary \nanalysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing. \n \n \nIII.  Results \nA. Baselined Activities Completed \u2013 Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight \nThe first round of Flame Design tests, during which a total of 188 flames were ignited, was \nconducted during Increments 59-60, more specifically from April-July.  These tests \nfeatured normal flames, where gaseous fuel was ejected from a porous spherical burner \ninto an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere.  In contrast, the upcoming second round will \nfeature tests with inverse flames, where the oxidizer is ejected from the burner into a \nfuel/nitrogen atmosphere.  As a result, the second round flames will be \u2018inside out.\u2019  It is \ncurrently expected that the second round of tests will be conducted during Increment 63.  \nThe \u2018interruption\u2019 in the Flame Design testing was intentionally planned years in advance \nto enable the PI team to review the initial results in detail and with that knowledge plan \nthe second round of tests to maximize its scientific outcome.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 34}}